import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
import { join } from 'node:path';
const runtime = process.env.OMGITHUB_PLAYWRIGHT_DIR || join(process.env.HOME, '.local/share/omgithub-playwright');
const require = createRequire(join(runtime, 'package.json'));
const { chromium } = require('playwright');
const browser = await chromium.launch({ executablePath: '/opt/google/chrome/chrome', headless: false, args: ['--enable-gpu', '--ignore-gpu-blocklist'] });
const ctx = await browser.newContext({ viewport: { width: 390, height: 844 }, screen: { width: 390, height: 844 }, deviceScaleFactor: 1, isMobile: true, hasTouch: true });
const page = await ctx.newPage();
await page.goto('http://localhost:3002', { waitUntil: 'domcontentloaded' });
await page.waitForFunction(() => window.__apex && getComputedStyle(document.querySelector('#loading')).display === 'none', null, { timeout: 30000 });
await page.click('#btn-garage');
await page.waitForTimeout(1500);
const THREE = await page.evaluate(() => !!window.__apex.showroom);
console.log('showroom', THREE);
await page.evaluate(() => {
  const s = window.__apex.showroom;
  const THREE = s.scene.children.find(c => c.isMesh)?.constructor; // not used
  // find module constructors via a known object
  const light = Object.getPrototypeOf(s.scene.children.find(c => c.isHemisphereLight)).constructor;
});
// Add fill using the constructors exposed by existing objects
const info = await page.evaluate(() => {
  const s = window.__apex.showroom;
  const scene = s.scene;
  const hemi = scene.children.find(c => c.isHemisphereLight);
  const HemiCtor = hemi.constructor;
  const fill = new HemiCtor(0xffe6d5, 0x20242a, 1.6);
  fill.name = 'test-fill';
  scene.add(fill);
  return { hemiIntensity: hemi.intensity, added: !!scene.getObjectByName('test-fill') };
});
console.log(info);
await page.waitForTimeout(500);
await page.screenshot({ path: '/tmp/opencode/v-garage-mobile-fill.jpg', type: 'jpeg', quality: 62 });
await browser.close();

content.text:
Adding a hemisphere fill makes a huge difference — the car is now bright red and clearly visible. This should be a real fix. Add a portrait-friendly fill light in createShowroom that scales with aspect. But the HemiCtor fill at intensity 1.6 might wash the desktop scene. We can add a dedicated hemisphere light with intensity 0 in non-portrait and animate to 1.4 in portrait. Or simply raise the existing hemi from 0.55 to ~1.1 always; desktop might become too bright, though the desktop scene already looks great with strong contrast. Let's test a separate fill controlled by aspect to be safe.

Implement in world.js:
```
const portraitFill = new THREE.HemisphereLight(0xffe6d5, 0x20242a, 0);
scene.add(portraitFill);
```
In update:
```
portraitFill.intensity = portrait ? 1.5 : 0;
```
Maybe 1.2 for balance. The test at 1.6 looked good, slightly bright but okay. Let's choose 1.25 and also keep key. Actually the car color #C90D19 looked vibrant. Use 1.3.

But this fill also affects garage desktop? intensity 0 there. Good.

Also menu on mobile: portrait menu mode will also get fill, making the car visible if it's in frame. Let's check whether mobile menu shows car — with fill it might appear behind content. Could be nice. But the menu camera is fixed and car might be out of frame. We could adjust the menu camera for portrait to show the car at bottom/behind. But menu design on mobile intentionally hides the car; not necessary.

Now landscape touch controls: Let's inspect the CSS around hud and landscape. If I can improve ergonomics, do it. Current layout at 844x390: touch-controls right side row. I propose for `(pointer: coarse) and (max-height: 520px)`:
- Keep `.touch-controls` spanning left/right, justify-content space-between, bottom 10px.
- Put `.touch-steer` at left, `.touch-pedals` at right.
- Hide the speedo gauge/tuck it? The speedo currently bottom-left conflicts with steer. We can hide `.speedo-arc` and `.throttle-strip`, keep speed number compact at top or above the steering. But bottom-left is most natural for steering. Move speedo to top-right? HUD timing is top-right. Maybe place steering above the speedo (bottom: 130px, left 12px) and keep pedals bottom-right (bottom 14px). In landscape 390px height, bottom 120px would put steering at y~212-270; that's over the track but okay. The speedo is at bottom ~250-390. Buttons at 212-270 might overlap leaderboard at left (ends ~260). Hmm.

Alternative: hide speedo entirely in landscape (minimap already hidden), and show speed as text in HUD timing area? The HUD already has speed display in speedo; hiding loses speed/gear. Could keep just the readout (no arc) at bottom center-left.

Actually the current all-right layout is playable: using the right thumb for gas/brake and left thumb... wait both groups are on the right, so left thumb is free. In landscape, thumbs hold both sides of device; left thumb could steer but buttons are far right (x 590-830). Left thumb can't reach across. So steering and pedals both need right thumb, impossible simultaneously. But since auto-throttle, gas is optional; braking needs right thumb and steering too. This is a genuine usability issue.

Better: Move steering to bottom-left. Hide or relocate speedo. For landscape:
```
@media (pointer: coarse) and (max-height: 520px) {
  .minimap-panel { display:none; }
  .hud-bottom { bottom: 10px; left: 12px; right: 12px; }
  .speedo { transform: scale(.62); transform-origin: bottom left; opacity: .9; }  // occupies less
  .touch-controls { left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 8px; }
  .touch-steer { position: absolute; left: 12px; bottom: 0; }
  .touch-pedals { position: absolute; right: 12px; bottom: 0; }
}
```
But speedo bottom-left under steer. Could move speedo to top-center? The HUD top has position/leaderboard left, timing center, pause right. In landscape, there is a gap at bottom center. We can place speedo bottom center (left: 50%; transform translateX(-50%)) with scale .7, above or below controls? Bottom center at 390 height: speedo is 124 tall; place bottom 6 → y 260-384, controls at bottom 14 in corners, no overlap (controls x 12-138 on left; speedo centered at x 300-540). Good. So:

- `.touch-controls` left/right full width, bottom 14, justify-content space-between.
- `.speedo` position bottom center with smaller scale, maybe left 50%, transform translateX(-50%) scale(.72), transform-origin bottom center.
- Hide throttle strip (already inside speedo). 
- Gear can remain.

Wait the `.hud-bottom` also contains minimap; hiding minimap leaves only speedo. `.hud-bottom` is display flex justify-between probably. Let me inspect CSS for hud-bottom/speedo.

